# Audience: explanation level (project owner)

The project owner is **not** doing day-to-day implementation. Treat chat explanations accordingly.

## Default (unless they ask to go deeper)

- Prioritize **what happened**, **why** (cause, assumptions, tradeoffs), and **what it means** for the product or workflow — in **plain language**, not a code tour.
- Skip step-by-step walkthroughs of functions, line numbers, and identifier-heavy narration unless they ask or it is needed to avoid a wrong fix.
- When they may want detail later, **point to the right place**: e.g. `docs/architecture.md`, `practices/project_practices.md`, `docs/todo/<target>.md`, or the relevant subsystem path — without pasting large excerpts.

## When to add more technical detail

- They ask (e.g. “show the code”, “which file”, “line level”).
- A small code pointer is the clearest way to answer “where do I look?” — then prefer **file or subsystem + one sentence**, not a full walkthrough.
- The task is **implementing or debugging code** in-repo: still do correct engineering work; summarize to the owner in the default style above, and keep deep dives in chat optional.

## What this does not change

- **Commits and PR-style notes** stay as the repo rules require (those are for history and collaborators, not chat tone).
- **Code quality and correctness** are unchanged; only default **user-facing explanation depth** shifts.
